            {
                "entry_id": "93ee39ac-cd27-32e8-96a2-2129552b7c72",
                "word": "ghréline",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "d85b4d55-2b1d-4f1b-95cb-4edcd66afc37",
                        "entry_id": "93ee39ac-cd27-32e8-96a2-2129552b7c72",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "ghrélines"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5071e1a9-9c17-394a-a8a4-afd4b961897b",
                        "entry_id": "93ee39ac-cd27-32e8-96a2-2129552b7c72",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Hormone qui stimule l’appétit, sécrétée principalement par les cellules du fundus de l’estomac, et par les cellules Epsilon du pancréas, ainsi que dans le noyau arqué de l’hypothalamus moyen, augmentant avant les repas et diminuant après ceux-ci."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4840f1fd-0605-482d-a2a3-f09351274e94",
                                "sense_id": "5071e1a9-9c17-394a-a8a4-afd4b961897b",
                                "text": "[…] En cause? un dérèglement dans la production de deux hormones : la leptine, qui inhibe l'appétit, et la ghréline, qui augmente la sensation de faim. En cas de carence de sommeil, le taux de la première chute.tandis que celui de la seconde explose."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "cbe57e1c-c56a-4b93-ada8-43a3164bc24a",
                        "source_entry_id": "93ee39ac-cd27-32e8-96a2-2129552b7c72",
                        "source_sense_id": "5071e1a9-9c17-394a-a8a4-afd4b961897b",
                        "target_lang_word": "ghrelin",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "ghrelin",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "06b6f35d-7b33-3611-b39e-fff9ea357fbe",
                                        "entry_id": "775d3175-7211-3b34-9bd7-021aa92693fd",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A peptide hormone, secreted in the stomach when empty, that increases appetite and secretion of growth hormone from the pituitary gland."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable",
                                            "usually"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3a71a34b-8390-43b6-bb22-302418d745da",
                                                "sense_id": "06b6f35d-7b33-3611-b39e-fff9ea357fbe",
                                                "text": "We designate the GH-releasing peptide ‘ghrelin’ (ghre is the Proto-Indo-European root of the word ‘grow’)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "63621cd5-4875-4b29-ae93-90b284f9b679",
                                                "sense_id": "06b6f35d-7b33-3611-b39e-fff9ea357fbe",
                                                "text": "Both human and rat ghrelins are 28 amino acid peptides, in which Ser3 is modified by a fatty acid, primarily n-octanoic acid."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ccdd7c93-8cf0-433c-bb84-30dfbea269c7",
                                                "sense_id": "06b6f35d-7b33-3611-b39e-fff9ea357fbe",
                                                "text": "[W]hen you increase ghrelin levels, you stimulate that growth hormone to kick in, and growth hormone builds you not only up but out as well. Your stomach secretes ghrelin in pulses every half hour, sending subtle chemical impulses to your brain—almost like subliminal biological messages (carrot cake, carrot cake, carrot cake)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f4bbfb8a-ca1e-4b92-83c8-5dce57d99d9d",
                                                "sense_id": "06b6f35d-7b33-3611-b39e-fff9ea357fbe",
                                                "text": "Ghrelin is a 28‐amino acid acylated peptide predominantly secreted by the stomach but also by the brain. In mammals, ghrelin stimulates both GH [growth hormone] secretion and appetite, and it is the only GI [gastrointestinal] hormone with confirmed orexigenic properties[…]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "27134698-4a39-48f2-9cbb-c01378343dac",
                                                "sense_id": "06b6f35d-7b33-3611-b39e-fff9ea357fbe",
                                                "text": "Currently, ghrelin is considered as a “hunger hormone” that signals the brain the need to initiate food consumption. […] After food ingestion, plasma levels of ghrelin drastically decrease. Some but not all studies provided the evidence that in the cephalic phase of food ingestion the vagal efferent system promotes ghrelin secretion[…]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2b0327bf-a895-4199-afe0-664053abbb5f",
                                                "sense_id": "06b6f35d-7b33-3611-b39e-fff9ea357fbe",
                                                "text": "That is because sleep affects two appetite-controlling hormones, leptin and ghrelin."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "300c6989-8ff4-4dcb-988c-2725d7172262",
